The last line in the contract test is for an assertion using Debug.Assert. The purpose is to prevent the unit test from running if the return value of arrList.Contains(x) is false while the size of the original ArrayList and the new ArrayList are the same. 
Can you find out what is its function or what does it do?

A: It seems like you're talking about an assertion used in a contract test. 
Assert is a statement which checks a certain condition. If the condition is not met, a failure of the assertion is reported. In this case, the condition is not met when arrList.Contains(x) is false AND the sizes of the original ArrayList (old arrList) and the new ArrayList (new arrList) are same. 
In the context of these tests, the assertion tries to catch a scenario where a Remove operation is performed on an item that doesn't exist in the list. The assumption here is that if you tried to remove an item that doesn't exist in a list, the count of the list should remain the same. Therefore, it's trying to enforce that after trying to remove an item, the count of the list must be equal to its original count. And, since Remove operation didn't succeed in such scenario (item doesn't exist), it assumes arrList.Contains(x) returns False. 
The Debug.Assert command is useful for catching the state of the program if it deviates from the expected state, similar to setting a breakpoint on debugging, but it is not a unit testing tool, it is part of the .NET framework. 
Remember, these Assert statements should generally be removed or disabled in production code, as they are added to debug code and cannot be optimized out by the compiler."
Q: Create a list of strings from a list of tuples in Python I'm trying to create a list of strings from a list of tuples in Python, so far I've come up with this line of code which works, but I'm not sure it's the best practice, as it looks clumsy and somewhat of a one-off solution.
list_of_strings = [str(item) for item in list_of_tuples]

The list_of_tuples variable is a list of tuples, for instance:
list_of_tuples = [(5, 'hello'), (7, 'world')]

And I want to convert each tuple to string so that my new list looks like:
list_of_strings = ['(5, hello)', '(7, world)']

Any better and more elegant solutions to accomplish the same goal?

A: I think you are on the right track with the comprehension. However, if you want the tuples as string, then you can get those results with astype:
list_of_strings = [str(t) for t in list_of_tuples] 

If you do it with astype:
list_of_strings = list_of_tuples.astype(str)

This would return a list where each tuple element has been cast to a string. 
Both solutions would give you: ['(5, hello)', '(7, world)']

A: Both of yours are Pythonic ways to convert a list of tuples into a list of strings. Both of them are efficient and neat. Just remember to convert your tuples to strings to display them as strings.

A: In addition to the answers already given, you can also use map() function to convert each tuple to a string:
list_of_strings = list(map(str, list_of_tuples))

Also, if you have any concern about the efficiency of these methods, here is how you can go around it:
import timeit
tuples = [(1,2,3),(4,5,6),(7,8,9)]
%timeit [str(t) for t in tuples]
%timeit list(map(str, tuples))

This will give you a fair comparison between map and list comprehensions. Please take note, map() function in Python 3 returns an iterable map object which needs to be converted into list to see the results.
On average, both methods will have comparable time complexity of--------------------
        }

